(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-3-cms-exam-3-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 3 Master Exam — Form D","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 48-year-old has recurrent painful swelling of the left submandibular gland that comes on within minutes of eating and settles over an hour. The duct orifice is inflamed and no stone is palpable. Imaging is arranged to find the calculus. How do the imaging modalities compare for detecting a salivary calculus?","choices":["Computed tomography most sensitive, sialography most accurate","Magnetic resonance imaging is the most sensitive","Plain radiography is the most sensitive","Ultrasound is both the most sensitive and the most accurate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. These two claims are separate. Computed tomography detects the greatest proportion of stones, because it resolves small calcifications anywhere in the gland and duct, while digital subtraction sialography is described as the most accurate method for detecting calculi because it opacifies the duct system directly. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy has had two weeks of purulent nasal discharge. He has been afebrile with no respiratory symptoms. His mother says he gets into everything. Examination shows right-sided purulent discharge that is greenish-brown and extraordinarily foul-smelling. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Culture and sensitivity of the nasal discharge","Direct visualization of the right nasal vestibule","Sinus radiographs","Soft tissue lateral radiograph of the nasopharynx"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unilateral, foul-smelling purulent discharge in a young child is a retained nasal foreign body until proven otherwise, because an obstructing object traps secretions that stagnate and become malodorous. Looking directly into the vestibule both makes the diagnosis and allows the object to be removed, which is the treatment. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has recurrent painful lesions at the vermilion border of the lip, always preceded by a day of burning and tingling, triggered by sunlight and stress. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["A new primary infection each time","An autoimmune reaction to sunlight","Bacterial superinfection of chapped lips","Reactivation of virus latent in the trigeminal ganglion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. After a primary infection the virus travels along the axon to the trigeminal ganglion and persists there. Reactivation is triggered by stress, trauma, immunosuppression or ultraviolet light, and the virus migrates back down the axonal sheath, which is why the prodrome of burning and tingling precedes any visible lesion by about 24 hours. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 2-year-old has had recurrent middle ear effusions for a year and hearing has been reduced throughout. The child has fewer words than expected for age but is normal in every other respect. The parent asks why this is being treated more urgently than the same problem would be in an adult. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Children cannot tolerate hearing aids","Children have more fragile cochlear hair cells","Conductive loss becomes permanent if untreated in children","Language acquisition depends on hearing during a limited window"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Speech and language develop during a critical period in early childhood, and reduced auditory input during that window produces delays that persist even after hearing is restored. An adult with the same conductive loss is inconvenienced; a toddler may lose ground that is harder to recover, which is why the threshold to intervene is lower. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man completes a course of intravenous gentamicin for a severe infection. He now reports bilateral high-frequency hearing loss and persistent tinnitus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A reversible effect that will resolve once the drug clears","Aminoglycoside ototoxicity, which is often irreversible","Noise-induced loss from the hospital environment","Presbycusis unmasked by the illness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides accumulate in cochlear hair cells and damage them, starting with the outer hair cells at the cochlear base which encode high frequencies. Because mammalian hair cells do not regenerate, the loss is typically permanent, which is why monitoring during treatment matters more than treatment afterwards. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes has had left ear pain and discharge for three weeks despite two courses of topical drops. The pain is now severe, worse at night, and there is granulation tissue at the bony-cartilaginous junction of the canal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Cholesteatoma","Malignant (necrotising) otitis externa","Otomycosis","Simple bacterial otitis externa"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Diabetes and immunocompromise are the setting, and the combination of pain out of proportion, failure of topical therapy, and granulation tissue at the bony-cartilaginous junction is the classic description. The infection has left the skin and entered bone as a skull base osteomyelitis, usually Pseudomonas, which is why it needs systemic therapy and imaging rather than more drops. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman has had four days of nasal congestion, clear-to-yellow discharge and facial pressure worse when she bends forward, following a cold. She is afebrile and feels gradually better than two days ago. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Computed tomography of the sinuses","Immediate amoxicillin-clavulanate","Sinus puncture for culture","Symptomatic treatment without antibiotics"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Between 90 and 98 percent of acute rhinosinusitis is viral, and the features that would suggest bacterial infection are absent: it has been under ten days, she is improving rather than showing double worsening, and she is afebrile. Discharge color does not indicate bacterial infection, so the treatment is decongestants, analgesia and saline. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman has sudden severe vertigo with vomiting. She is unable to sit unsupported, has a severe occipital headache, and there is direction-changing nystagmus. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Cerebellar infarction","Labyrinthitis","Vestibular neuronitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Three findings each argue against a peripheral cause: truncal ataxia so severe she cannot sit, a severe headache, and direction-changing nystagmus. Peripheral vertigo produces unidirectional nystagmus and patients who are unsteady but can usually sit. This combination requires urgent imaging because the posterior fossa can swell and compress the brainstem. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 97"},{"q":"A newborn has a large firm neck mass noted at delivery. Imaging shows calcifications within it, and the infant has stridor and difficulty feeding. The mass does not transilluminate and has not changed in size since birth. What is the most likely diagnosis, and how is it managed?","choices":["Hemangioma, which will involute","Lymphangioma, managed with sclerotherapy","Teratoma, requiring surgical excision","Thyroglossal duct cyst"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Head and neck teratomas account for about 3.5 percent of all teratomas, originate from pluripotent cells, and are most commonly noted at birth or within the first year. The calcifications reflect the varied tissue types a teratoma contains, and when large enough these masses cause respiratory compromise or dysphagia, which is why excision is needed. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 58-year-old has brief episodes of spinning triggered by rolling over in bed, each lasting under a minute. Hearing is normal and there are no neurological signs. On the Dix-Hallpike maneuver the nystagmus begins after a brief delay, lasts under a minute and diminishes on repetition. What does this indicate?","choices":["A central cause requiring urgent imaging","A normal result","A peripheral cause, consistent with positional vertigo","An inconclusive result requiring electronystagmography"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Latency before onset, a short duration and fatigability on repeat testing are the signature of otoconia moving within a semicircular canal and then settling. Central positional nystagmus, by contrast, begins immediately, persists and does not fatigue, which is what makes these three features worth noting explicitly. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 88"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman has a firm thyroid nodule with a hoarse voice. The clinician is deciding which imaging to request, and cancer is suspected. Which imaging is most appropriate?","choices":["Contrast computed tomography of the neck","Plain radiography of the neck","Positron emission tomography","Ultrasound, avoiding iodine-containing contrast"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Ultrasound characterizes thyroid nodules well without radiation and guides aspiration. The specific trap is contrast computed tomography: an iodine load saturates the thyroid and compromises subsequent radioiodine treatment, so it is avoided whenever thyroid cancer is on the differential. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 34-year-old reports that conversation is clearer in a noisy restaurant than in a quiet room, which family members find hard to believe. Hearing has declined gradually since the patient's second pregnancy. Otoscopy is normal. What does this symptom suggest?","choices":["A conductive loss","A retrocochlear lesion","A sensorineural loss","Normal hearing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. This is paracusis, and it happens for two reasons that reinforce each other. A conductive block filters out low-frequency ambient noise more effectively than it filters speech, and everyone raises their voice in a noisy room. Sensorineural patients experience the exact opposite, which makes this symptom a useful discriminator. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 59-year-old is starting a six-week course of intravenous gentamicin for endocarditis. Hearing and balance are currently normal. The clinician enrolls the patient in monitoring for cochlear toxicity for the duration of treatment. What should the program involve?","choices":["Baseline and serial audiometry","Monthly tympanometry","Symptom review alone","Weekly tuning fork testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aminoglycosides damage the outer hair cells at the cochlear base, which encode the highest frequencies, so loss appears there before it reaches the speech range. Detecting it early allows the drug to be changed before the patient notices any functional problem, which matters because the damage is usually permanent. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 25-year-old patient has acute otitis media with a serous effusion in the right ear. Audiometry confirms a marked conductive impairment on that side. Weber and Rinne tests are performed. Which set of tuning fork findings would be expected?","choices":["Weber is equal in both ears; bone conduction exceeds air conduction on the right","Weber lateralizes to the left; bone conduction exceeds air conduction on the right","Weber lateralizes to the right; air conduction exceeds bone conduction on the right","Weber lateralizes to the right; bone conduction exceeds air conduction on the right"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fluid in the middle ear blocks air conduction but leaves the cochlea and the skull's own transmission untouched. Weber therefore lateralizes TOWARD the affected ear, because the blocked ear is not competing with ambient room noise and hears the bone-conducted tone better. In the same ear Rinne reverses: bone now beats air, which never happens in a normal or sensorineural ear. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man with newly diagnosed HIV has generalized cervical lymphadenopathy with no identifiable infectious or neoplastic cause after investigation. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Idiopathic follicular hyperplasia requiring excision","Kaposi sarcoma","Persistent generalized lymphadenopathy, treated by treating the HIV","Tuberculous adenitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Persistent generalized lymphadenopathy is defined by the absence of an identifiable infectious or neoplastic cause, and the neck is its commonest site. It reflects the immune activation of untreated infection, which is why the treatment is antiretroviral therapy rather than anything directed at the nodes themselves. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has papillary thyroid carcinoma confined to one lobe, well differentiated, with no evidence of metastasis. What is the appropriate extent of surgery?","choices":["Chemotherapy and radiation rather than surgery","Isthmectomy is the appropriate operation","Lobectomy may be sufficient rather than total thyroidectomy","Total thyroidectomy is mandatory in all thyroid cancer"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Almost all thyroid cancers require thyroidectomy, with one explicit exception: disease localized to one lobe, well differentiated and without metastasis. That is exactly this patient, and lobectomy preserves the contralateral gland and reduces the risk to the recurrent laryngeal nerves and parathyroids. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 17-year-old attended four days ago with a 101.8 degree Fahrenheit fever and was diagnosed with acute pharyngitis and given penicillin. The sore throat is now worse, they cannot drink, and swallowing is excruciating. The voice is muffled. Examination shows right medial deviation of the soft palate with marked right tonsillar swelling. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dental abscess","Mononucleosis","Oral candidiasis","Peritonsillar abscess"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pus collecting between the tonsillar capsule and the pharyngeal muscles pushes the soft palate and tonsil medially, which produces the deviation described. The muffled hot potato voice comes from the mass distorting the oropharynx, and the classic triad is completed by trismus, the most reliable symptom. It is the commonest deep infection of the head and neck and typically follows an inadequately treated tonsillitis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 99"},{"q":"A 24-year-old woman is struck in the nose. She has swelling, an obviously deviated nose, and cannot breathe through the left nostril. What is the most appropriate imaging?","choices":["Computed tomography of the facial skeleton as first line","Immediate closed reduction without assessment","No imaging is needed, as nasal fractures are managed clinically","Radiography is indicated, because she fails the clinical criteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Imaging can be omitted only when all four reassuring criteria are met: tenderness confined to the bony bridge, patent airflow through each nostril, a straight nose with no septal deviation, and no septal hematoma. She fails two of them, so the criteria no longer permit a clinical-only approach and the nose has to be imaged and assessed for reduction. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 62-year-old smoker has a firm cervical node. Fine needle aspiration shows squamous cell carcinoma, but no primary tumor is apparent on initial examination. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Examine all mucosal surfaces of the head and neck","Proceed directly to neck dissection","Repeat the aspiration to confirm the cell type","Treat empirically with radiotherapy to the neck"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma is the commonest metastatic lesion to the neck, and it arrives from the skin or the upper aerodigestive tract. A systematic office examination of every mucosal surface, the thyroid, the salivary glands and the skin usually finds the primary, so that comes before more imaging. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 3-year-old inserted a small button battery into the nose two hours ago. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Irrigation of the nasal cavity to flush the object out","Observation, since most nasal foreign bodies pass spontaneously","Routine removal at the next available clinic appointment","Urgent removal, because a battery causes rapid liquefactive necrosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A button battery generates an electrical current across moist mucosa, producing hydroxide ions and liquefactive necrosis that can perforate the septum within hours. That makes it the one nasal foreign body treated as a true emergency, unlike inert beads or foam where the damage is obstruction and secondary infection over days. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 7-year-old with four days of purulent rhinorrhea develops swelling and redness of the right eyelids, pain on eye movement and mild proptosis. Computed tomography shows opacification of the ethmoid sinuses with a subperiosteal collection in the orbit. Why does the ethmoid sinus in particular threaten the eye?","choices":["The ethmoid sinus drains directly into the orbit","The frontal sinus shares a wall with the globe","The lamina papyracea separating them is paper-thin","The maxillary sinus is closer to the orbit than the ethmoid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The medial orbital wall is formed by the lamina papyracea, literally the papery layer, and it is the thinnest bone in the orbit. Infection in the ethmoid air cells has only that barrier to cross, which is why ethmoid sinusitis is the commonest sinus source of orbital cellulitis, particularly in children. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has a slowly enlarging painless mass at the tail of the parotid gland with no facial weakness. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A benign parotid tumor, most likely a pleomorphic adenoma","A malignant parotid tumor, most likely mucoepidermoid carcinoma","A minor salivary gland tumor","A parotid abscess"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Between 64 and 80 percent of salivary neoplasms arise in the parotid, and 75 to 80 percent of parotid tumors are benign. Benign parotid tumors characteristically present as slow-growing painless masses at the tail of the gland, and the absence of facial nerve involvement supports a benign process. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 141"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman has had three weeks of intense itching in the right ear with only mild pain, having finished two courses of antibiotic ear drops for otitis externa. The canal contains white curd-like debris with fine black specks. What is the most likely diagnosis, and how should it be treated?","choices":["Chronic suppurative otitis media","Contact dermatitis from the drops, treated with topical steroid","Otomycosis, treated by cleaning the canal and applying an antifungal","Resistant bacterial otitis externa needing a stronger antibiotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Prolonged antibiotic drops suppress the canal's bacterial flora and let Aspergillus or Candida take over, which is why this follows treatment rather than preceding it. Itch dominating over pain and the visible fungal debris are the clues, and treatment is thorough cleaning to remove the fungal mass plus a topical antifungal. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 44"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with chronic suppurative otitis media asks why her ear discharges but does not hurt. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Chronic infection destroys the nerve supply to the drum","The discharge contains anesthetic breakdown products","The organisms involved do not produce inflammation","The perforation lets the middle ear drain, so pressure never builds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pain in acute otitis media comes from pus stretching an intact drum. With an established perforation the middle ear has a permanent outlet, so pressure cannot accumulate and the infection expresses itself as discharge rather than pain. That painlessness is the clinical hallmark separating chronic from acute disease. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 4-year-old boy is brought in after putting a small plastic bead in his right ear canal. He is cooperative, the bead is smooth, round and visible in the outer third of the canal, and the tympanic membrane beyond it appears intact. What is the most appropriate method of removal?","choices":["Grasping the bead with alligator forceps","Irrigation of the canal with warm water","Referral for removal under general anesthesia as the first step","Removal under direct vision with an instrument passed beyond the object"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A smooth round object cannot be grasped by forceps because they simply push it deeper, so the technique is to pass a hook or curette past it under direct vision and draw it outward. Direct visualization matters because the risk of the procedure is pushing the object against or through the drum. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with hypertension and atrial fibrillation has episodes of vertigo accompanied by diplopia, dysarthria and transient numbness of one arm. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Meniere disease","Vertebrobasilar insufficiency","Vestibular neuronitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vertigo accompanied by other brainstem signs indicates the posterior circulation rather than the labyrinth, because the same vessels supply the vestibular nuclei, the cranial nerve nuclei and the long tracts. Diplopia, dysarthria and limb numbering alongside vertigo is the combination that moves the diagnosis centrally in a vascular patient. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man has a fluctuant, tender neck mass with overlying erythema and fever after a sore throat. Antibiotics have not settled it over 72 hours. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Change to a broader-spectrum antibiotic and continue","Excisional biopsy of the node","Fine needle aspiration or incision and drainage","Observation, since most nodes settle spontaneously"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Suppurative bacterial lymphadenopathy is treated empirically with antibiotics covering anaerobes and gram-positive organisms, but aspiration or incision and drainage follows when antibiotics fail. Fluctuance means there is a collection, and a collection needs draining rather than more antibiotic. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy who shouts constantly during sport has been hoarse for four months. Laryngoscopy shows small, well-defined, whitish, bilateral and symmetric lesions at the junction of the anterior third and posterior two thirds of the vocal folds. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Antireflux therapy","Inhaled corticosteroid","Speech therapy","Surgical excision of both nodules"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Vocal cord nodules are the commonest cause of persistent dysphonia in children, sometimes called screamers' nodules, and they form where the folds strike each other hardest during phonation. Because they are caused by mechanical trauma from vocal behavior, speech therapy that changes that behavior is first-line treatment in both children and adults. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 47"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman has lacy white lines on both buccal mucosae that cannot be wiped away. She asks whether it is dangerous. What should the patient be told?","choices":["It carries a 1 to 4 percent risk of carcinoma","It is an infection that will clear with antifungal treatment","It is entirely benign and needs no follow-up","It transforms to malignancy in the majority of cases"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Oral lichen planus is a chronic autoimmune condition in which activated lymphocytes destroy the basal layer, and between 1 and 4 percent progress to squamous cell carcinoma. The risk is higher with ulcerative lesions, which is why close follow-up rather than simple reassurance is the point of making the diagnosis. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 14-month-old has had three episodes of acute otitis media since starting daycare, while the child's father has never had one. The parent asks why young children get these infections so much more often than adults do. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["Children have larger middle ear cavities","Children have thinner tympanic membranes","Children produce more cerumen, blocking the canal","The child's tube is shorter, wider and flatter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The adult tube runs downward at an angle, so gravity assists drainage and its narrower caliber resists reflux from the nasopharynx. In a young child it is short, wide and nearly horizontal, which lets nasopharyngeal secretions and organisms reach the middle ear easily and drain from it poorly. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with a conductive loss from otosclerosis is choosing between stapedectomy and a hearing aid. What should the patient be told?","choices":["Both are reasonable; surgery addresses the mechanism, an aid amplifies","Neither will help until the loss becomes sensorineural","Only a hearing aid is appropriate, as surgery is obsolete","Only surgery can help a conductive loss"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In otosclerosis the cochlea works normally and the problem is purely mechanical, so stapedectomy can restore hearing by bypassing the fixed footplate. A hearing aid achieves a functional result without operative risk, which includes the small chance of a sensorineural loss. The choice depends on how the patient weighs those. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man has had a dry central perforation for eight months following an infection. His hearing is reduced and he cannot swim. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Continue to keep the ear dry and review in a further year","Prescribe topical antibiotic drops","Reassure that hearing will return once the ear is fully healed","Refer for consideration of tympanoplasty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Most traumatic and post-infective perforations close within weeks to a few months. One that remains open at eight months with a persistent conductive loss and lifestyle restriction is unlikely to heal spontaneously, and surgical repair both restores hearing and closes the route by which water reaches the middle ear. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has had nasal congestion, facial pressure and reduced smell continuously for five months, with intermittent flares. He has had three courses of antibiotics. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Allergic rhinitis alone","Chronic rhinosinusitis, beyond 12 weeks","Nasopharyngeal carcinoma","Recurrent acute bacterial sinusitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The dividing line between acute and chronic disease is twelve weeks of continuous symptoms, and five months is well beyond it. The mechanism shifts from a single infection to impaired mucociliary clearance with persistent inflammation, which is why repeated antibiotic courses treat the flares without changing the underlying problem. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman has a mildly deviated septum found incidentally. She has no symptoms. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A long-term intranasal steroid","Annual imaging to monitor progression","No treatment, as an asymptomatic deviation needs none","Septoplasty to prevent future problems"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Septal deviation is extremely common and is only treated when it causes symptoms: obstruction, recurrent epistaxis from the drier open side, obstructive sleep apnea, snoring or facial pain. Operating on an incidental finding in an asymptomatic patient offers only the risks of surgery. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 29-year-old woman has had a blocked, full sensation in both ears since a head cold two weeks ago, with mild hearing reduction and a popping sensation on swallowing. Otoscopy shows retracted tympanic membranes. Tympanometry is performed. Which tympanometry result would be expected?","choices":["A type Ad tympanogram, with excessively deep compliance","A type As tympanogram, with reduced peak compliance","A type B tympanogram, flat across the sweep","A type C tympanogram, with the peak shifted to negative pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A eustachian tube that cannot ventilate the middle ear allows the trapped air to be absorbed, so middle ear pressure falls below atmospheric and the drum retracts. Tympanometry measures compliance across a pressure sweep, and the peak compliance shifts to wherever middle ear pressure actually is: negative, which is the definition of a type C trace. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 33-year-old is undergoing excision of a lymph node from the occipital triangle of the posterior neck. The surgeon warns that a nerve crossing the muscular floor of that triangle is at risk, and that injury would cause difficulty raising the arm above the head. Which nerve is it?","choices":["The hypoglossal nerve","The recurrent laryngeal nerve","The spinal accessory nerve","The vagus nerve"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The spinal accessory nerve crosses the muscular floor of the occipital triangle to pass deep to trapezius, which places it superficially and vulnerably in the posterior triangle. Injury there denervates trapezius and produces a painful drooping shoulder, which is why it is the classic nerve at risk in posterior triangle surgery. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 5-year-old has ten days of purulent nasal discharge and cough that has not improved. He is afebrile and otherwise well. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["A nasal foreign body","A prolonged viral upper respiratory infection needing no treatment","Allergic rhinitis","Bacterial sinusitis, meeting the ten-day criterion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The same criteria apply in children as in adults, and persistence beyond ten days without improvement is one of them. Children have maxillary and ethmoid sinuses from birth, so sinusitis is possible at this age, and the ethmoid proximity to the orbit is what makes recognition important. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman has a thyroid nodule. Fine needle aspiration shows a well-differentiated carcinoma of thyroid epithelial cells. She is told this is the commonest type and has the best outlook. Which type is it?","choices":["Anaplastic carcinoma","Follicular carcinoma","Medullary carcinoma","Papillary carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Papillary carcinoma accounts for about 75 percent of thyroid cancers, arises from thyroid epithelial cells, is commonest in young women, and has the best prognosis of the four types. Its tendency is to spread to local cervical lymph nodes rather than hematogenously, which is part of why the outlook is favorable. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has bilateral high-pitched ringing in both ears, present for two years, alongside a symmetrical high-frequency sensorineural loss. There is no pulsatility and no vertigo. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A trial of vestibular suppressants","Angiography to look for a vascular cause","Reassurance, hearing aids and sound therapy","Urgent magnetic resonance imaging of the internal auditory canals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Bilateral non-pulsatile tinnitus accompanying a symmetrical sensorineural loss is the common form, generated centrally as the brain compensates for reduced input. Because the driver is the hearing loss, amplification often reduces the tinnitus, and sound therapy works by reducing the contrast between the tinnitus and silence. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 22-year-old was struck on the nose playing football two hours ago. The nose is mildly swollen but appears straight, there is no epistaxis now, and the patient feels the injury is trivial. What should be looked for before the patient is discharged?","choices":["Assess only whether the nose is straight","Check only for external bruising","Inspect the septum for a boggy swelling","Obtain a radiograph in every case"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A septal hematoma is easy to miss because attention goes to the bridge and to whether the nose looks straight, yet it is the one finding that destroys cartilage within days if not drained. Looking specifically at the septum, and feeling it, is what the four-criteria rule makes explicit. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with confirmed Meniere disease asks what she can do herself to reduce the frequency of her attacks. What advice should be given?","choices":["Avoid all physical activity during remission","Increase fluid and salt intake to maintain blood pressure","Perform Epley maneuvers at home","Restrict dietary salt, caffeine and alcohol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The underlying abnormality is endolymphatic hydrops, an excess of endolymph distending the membranous labyrinth. Reducing sodium load lowers fluid retention and therefore endolymph volume, and caffeine and alcohol are recognized triggers. These are the measures a patient can act on before any drug is added. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is assaulted and has clear fluid dripping from one nostril. He also has periorbital bruising on both sides. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A septal hematoma draining spontaneously","Allergic rhinitis unmasked by the injury","Normal nasal secretions from mucosal irritation","Suspect a cerebrospinal fluid leak from a skull base fracture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Clear rhinorrhea after significant facial or head trauma, particularly with bilateral periorbital bruising, raises the possibility that the cribriform plate has fractured and cerebrospinal fluid is draining through the nose. That converts a nasal injury into a neurosurgical problem and carries a risk of meningitis. — hughie Nose & Paranasal Sinuses, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 57-year-old smoker has been hoarse for seven weeks. He has no pain and no other symptoms. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["A course of antibiotics","A trial of proton pump inhibitor for reflux laryngitis","Refer to ear, nose and throat for laryngoscopy","Voice rest and reassurance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Voice disturbance lasting more than two weeks is defined as chronic laryngitis, and the teaching is explicit that this is not a true diagnosis. It is a description that obliges a search for an underlying cause, and laryngeal cancer and vocal cord polyps are the two that must be excluded, particularly in a smoker. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has had four episodes over three months of spinning vertigo lasting two to three hours, each preceded by a sense of fullness and roaring tinnitus in the left ear. His hearing fluctuates and an audiogram during an attack shows low-frequency sensorineural loss. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acoustic neuroma","Benign paroxysmal positional vertigo","Meniere disease","Vestibular neuronitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The defining tetrad is episodic vertigo lasting minutes to hours, fluctuating sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus and aural fullness, all in the same ear. Endolymphatic hydrops distends the membranous labyrinth, and the low-frequency pattern of loss is characteristic because the apex of the cochlea, which codes low frequencies, distends most. — 16. Disorders of Inner Ear, Slide 76"},{"q":"A 61-year-old smoker has a 2 cm ulcer on the tongue. The team is staging the tumor and must record whether it arose in the oral cavity or the oropharynx, as the two are staged and treated differently. Where is the boundary between them?","choices":["Anterior two thirds of tongue against posterior third","The lip marks the anterior boundary of the oral cavity","The soft palate belongs to the oral cavity","The two are treated as a single site for staging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The oral cavity includes the anterior two thirds of the tongue, buccal mucosa, floor of mouth, hard palate, upper and lower gingiva and retromolar trigone. The oropharynx is the posterior third of the tongue, palatine tonsil, soft palate and posterior pharyngeal wall. The distinction matters because the two behave differently and are treated differently. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 144"},{"q":"A 6-year-old has a smooth, painless 2 cm midline neck swelling that moves upward when the child swallows and when the tongue is protruded. It became briefly tender during a recent cold. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Branchial cleft cyst","Dermoid cyst","Lymphangioma","Thyroglossal duct cyst"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Thyroglossal duct cysts make up about a third of all congenital neck masses, which is why a midline neck lump in a child has a strong prior probability. Their location can vary, with some presenting lateral or as low as the thyroid gland, and those are the ones hard to distinguish from a branchial cleft cyst. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 23"},{"q":"A 19-year-old university student is treated with amoxicillin for a presumed streptococcal sore throat. Three days later she develops a widespread maculopapular rash over the trunk. Her tonsils are enlarged with exudate and she has bilateral cervical lymphadenopathy and fatigue. What is the most likely explanation?","choices":["She has a true penicillin allergy and must avoid the class for life","She has infectious mononucleosis, and the aminopenicillin triggered the rash","The rash is scarlet fever from her streptococcal infection","The rash is unrelated and should prompt a search for another cause"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Giving an aminopenicillin to someone with Epstein-Barr virus infection reliably provokes a widespread exanthem, and the sequence described is the classic route to the diagnosis: a sore throat treated as strep, a rash, and then the real cause. The triad of fever, tonsillar pharyngitis and cervical lymphadenopathy fits mononucleosis rather than streptococcus. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man who drinks unpasteurized milk from a local farm has fever, fatigue, malaise and generalized lymphadenopathy including the neck. What is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Azithromycin","Penicillin","Streptomycin","Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Brucellosis is transmitted by ingestion of unpasteurized milk and presents with total body lymphadenopathy, fever, fatigue and malaise, unlike the submandibular, jugulodigastric or preauricular nodes of suppurative lymphadenopathy and cat scratch disease. Diagnosis is by serology and cultures; management is trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole or tetracycline. — CMS I Neoplasms and Neck Masses, Slide 33"},{"q":"An 11-year-old who swims daily has a 3-day history of right ear pain, itch and scanty discharge. The canal is erythematous and edematous and the pinna is tender to move. Topical treatment is being selected. Which organisms must the treatment cover?","choices":["Candida and Aspergillus","Moraxella catarrhalis and Mycoplasma","Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus","Streptococcus pneumoniae and Haemophilus influenzae"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Water exposure raises the canal pH and macerates the skin, which favors Pseudomonas in particular, alongside Staphylococcus from the skin flora. Topical fluoroquinolones are chosen because they cover both, and that Pseudomonas coverage is why an oral antibiotic like amoxicillin would be inadequate. — CMS I Disorders of the External and Middle Ear, Slide 41"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman is four days after hip surgery and has poor oral intake. She develops unilateral painful swelling over the angle of the jaw with overlying erythema. Pus can be expressed from the duct opposite the upper second molar. What is the most appropriate next step in management?","choices":["Antiviral therapy for mumps","Immediate parotidectomy","Rehydration and antistaphylococcal antibiotics","Sialography to identify a stone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dehydration and reduced oral intake cause salivary stasis, and stasis lets bacteria ascend the parotid duct. Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest organism, and pus expressible from Stensen duct confirms the gland is infected. Treatment is rehydration, which restores flow, together with penicillinase-resistant gram-positive cover. — CMS I Disorders of the Oral Cavity, Salivary Glands, Slide 38"}]};
